    thinking: "**Defining System Components**\n\nOkay, I\'m diving into the architecture now. My focus is on breaking down the system into clean modules. I\'ve started defining essential components, specifically `src/types.ts`. Within this file, I\'m thinking about the patient\'s state and trajectory. I\'ve begun working with `TrajectoryType` to cover different categories like \'normal\', \'suspect\', \'pathological\', and \'custom\'.\n\n\n**Outlining Data Structures**\n\nNow I\'m digging deeper into the specific data structures. I\'m nailing down the structure of the CTG data sample. I\'m also outlining the detailed structure of the 21 CTG feature vector and the classification results. This includes properties like the class, probabilities, confidence, and associated descriptions. I\'m also defining an Alert object to manage escalation levels and clinical notes.\n\n\n**Detailing Communications & Simulator**\n\nI\'ve been mapping out the communication methods, including SMS and voice alerts, detailing their states. The duty roster entry structure is set, now covering roles, shifts, and priorities. Telemetry settings are also defined, including controls for simulation speed, audio, and Twilio configuration. Further, I\'m now exploring the simulator module. It\'s focused on high-fidelity CTG physiological signal generation, including various crucial features.\n\n\n**Elaborating Feature Extraction**\n\nI\'m now deep in the feature extraction process. I\'m focusing on `src/lib/featureExtractor.ts`, calculating the 21 CTG standard clinical features across various rolling windows. The goal is to match the UCI CTG dataset standard from Ayres-de-Campos et al. I\'m building detailed calculation helpers, and also plan to include explainability breakdowns for each feature.\n\n\n**Describing Clinical Scenarios**\n\nI\'m now cataloging potential clinical scenarios, like a normal baseline with accelerations. I\'m also considering elevated baselines with decelerations, and severe bradycardia. I\'m building out a library of these, so these become inputs that drive simulation and testing. This is making the system far more robust.\n\n**Developing Audio & Notifications**\n\nI\'m currently focused on the `audioTelemetry.ts` module, building a Web Audio API synthesizer. I\'m aiming for realistic FHR Doppler pulse audio and implementing distinct chime and alarm tones for different urgency levels. I\'m also integrating web push notifications, Twilio SMS/Voice dispatch emulation, and text-to-speech voice call simulation so judges can literally test and hear the automated hospital phone alert: *\"Emergency Alert for Bed 3, Patient Eleanor Vance: Pathological Fetal Heart Rate detected*.\"\n\n\n**Constructing User Interface**\n\nI\'m now building out the user interface components. I\'ve designed `src/components/Header.tsx`, including a live clock, shift status, active doctor badge, audio toggle, and navigation tabs. I\'m also working on `src/components/WardOverview.tsx`, a multi-bed card grid displaying patient data and real-time CTG traces. Further, the Hero component `LiveMonitorPanel.tsx` is nearly done.\n\n\n**Designing UI Components**\n\nI\'m now detailing the user interface components further. I\'ve designed a live readout box and telemetry bar for instantaneous BPM, morphology diagnosis, and signal quality. I\'m also building clinical intervention triggers, bedside action bars, and a duty roster. I\'m working on notification centers and a Twilio simulator for alert testing.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 9
      nanos: 284152486
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
